---
intent: >
  Grow the bundled default `brain.aide` template body from one section
  (`## Prose`) to three (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research
  hub`). The Playbook hub section moves out of the inline TS constant
  in `provisionBrain/index.ts` into the template's body. The Research
  hub section is brand-new content the user is shipping for the first
  time. Frontmatter shape, platform branching, and the existing prose
  body are all preserved unchanged.
---

## Project Structure

This is an additive/edit plan against an existing helper. The helper's
external surface is unchanged: one default-exported function with the
same signature. What changes is the bytes the function emits — the body
grows from one section to three.

```
src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/
├── plan.aide                     ← [this file, NEW — no prior plan existed
│                                    for this helper folder]
├── index.ts                      ← [edit] export default function
│                                    obsidianBrainAideTemplate(rootPath: string): string
│                                    signature unchanged. Behavior:
│                                      - frontmatter unchanged (`name` +
│                                        platform-branched `mcpServerConfig`)
│                                      - existing `## Prose` body unchanged,
│                                        sentinel phrases preserved
│                                      - new `## Playbook hub` section: moves
│                                        the `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` constant
│                                        from `../index.ts` into this template
│                                        body verbatim
│                                      - new `## Research hub` section:
│                                        brand-new content the user is
│                                        shipping for the first time, modeled
│                                        on the Playbook hub's structural
│                                        shape (H1 title, "How to Use This
│                                        Index" guidance, table skeleton,
│                                        empty Sections list)
└── index.test.ts                 ← [edit] new tests for the three-section
                                     body, presence of all required headings,
                                     verbatim round-trip of the new sections;
                                     existing prose-sentinel and platform-
                                     branching tests are unchanged in shape
```

The folder shape is unchanged. The two files (`index.ts`,
`index.test.ts`) get edited in place. No new helpers, no new
subfolders.

## Prerequisites

**`src/service/parseBrainAide/`** must export the widened
`ParseBrainAideResult.ok` shape (with `playbookHub` and `researchHub`
fields next to `prose`) AND the closed three-section body grammar
before this plan's tests can pass. Specifically:

- `parseBrainAideFromString(content: string)` returns `{ kind: "ok",
  config, prose, playbookHub, researchHub }` when the content carries
  exactly the three required sections.
- `parseBrainAideFromString(content)` returns `{ kind: "malformed-body",
  reason }` when any required section is absent — which is what
  this plan's test suite asserts NOT happening on the new template.
- The required heading vocabulary is exactly `## Prose`, `## Playbook
  hub`, `## Research hub` — the strings this template emits in its
  body must match those headings byte-for-byte.

If `parseBrainAide`'s body-grammar widening has not landed when this
plan executes, the round-trip tests will surface as `malformed-body`
on the new template (because the legacy single-section parser only
extracts `## Prose` and rejects bodies whose grammar has grown). The
orchestrator must execute `src/service/parseBrainAide/`'s plan first.

## Plan

### 1. Grow the template body to three sections

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md`

- [x] Edit `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts`.
      Function signature is unchanged: `default function
      obsidianBrainAideTemplate(rootPath: string): string`.

      The returned string keeps its current frontmatter and `## Prose`
      body verbatim. Two new sections append after the prose body, in
      this exact order:

      - **`## Playbook hub`** — content sourced verbatim from the
        legacy `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` constant currently declared in
        `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts`. The bytes are:

        ```
        # Coding Playbook

        ## Task Routing

        | Task domain | Section |
        |-------------|---------|

        ---

        ## How to Use This Index

        Read this note first. Each section links to a **section hub**
        that lists its notes with keywords. Navigate to the section
        relevant to your task, then drill into the specific notes you
        need. Do not read all sections — only the ones whose keywords
        match the work.

        ---

        ## Always Read First

        These notes are **required reading** for every task, regardless
        of which section you're working in:

        1. **[[your-conventions-note]]** — Add your naming, function
           ordering, and code hygiene conventions here.
        2. **[[your-folder-structure-note]]** — Add your folder layout
           and progressive disclosure conventions here.

        ---

        ## Sections

        ---

        ## Contents
        ```

        These bytes are CARRIED FORWARD from the legacy constant —
        they are not re-authored. The body content is the user's to
        edit; this template seeds it once. The implementor copies
        the bytes verbatim from the constant in `../index.ts` so a
        diff between the constant and this section is empty (the
        constant gets DELETED in `provisionBrain`'s plan, this is
        the new home).

        IMPORTANT: this section's body contains nested `##` headings
        (e.g. `## Task Routing`, `## How to Use This Index`,
        `## Sections`, `## Contents`). The parser's closed-vocabulary
        check rejects unknown TOP-LEVEL `## ` headings — but these
        are inside a body section, not at the top level. The parser's
        walker scans for `^## .+$` which would match these too, so
        the walker MUST distinguish "headings inside a section's
        body" from "section-boundary headings." The reasoning is
        that this template has been correctly authored to only put
        these nested headings AFTER a section's opening `## ` line
        and before the next section's `## ` line — they are not
        intended as section boundaries. The parser walks linearly
        and treats every `^## .+$` as a candidate boundary; the
        walker's closed-vocabulary check then rejects any candidate
        not in the required set. **For this template, that means
        every `^## .+$` line in the bytes must either be one of the
        three required headings OR not exist.**

        Resolution: replace the nested `## ` headings inside the
        Playbook hub body with `### ` (H3) so the walker does not
        treat them as top-level boundaries. Specifically, in the
        body bytes above:
        - `## Task Routing` → `### Task Routing`
        - `## How to Use This Index` → `### How to Use This Index`
        - `## Always Read First` → `### Always Read First`
        - `## Sections` → `### Sections`
        - `## Contents` → `### Contents`

        Same for any nested headings the Research hub section
        introduces below. The H1 (`# Coding Playbook`,
        `# Research`) stays as `# `; only `## ` becomes `### `.
        This keeps the closed-vocabulary check honest: every
        top-level `## ` in the template body IS a section
        boundary, by construction.

      - **`## Research hub`** — brand-new content modeled on the
        Playbook hub's structural shape. The intent is the same:
        seed an empty hub the user fills in with their own
        domain content. Bytes:

        ```
        # Research

        ### Domains

        | Domain | Notes |
        |--------|-------|

        ---

        ### How to Use This Index

        Research notes are filed by domain. Each subdirectory holds
        notes for a single domain; read the domain hub before
        drilling into individual notes. Do not flat-search across
        every domain — the structure is the navigation.

        ---

        ### Domain Hubs

        Add domain hubs here as your research grows. Each hub lives
        at `<domain>/<domain>.md` and lists its child notes with
        keywords.

        ---

        ### Contents
        ```

        Note: `### Domains`, `### How to Use This Index`,
        `### Domain Hubs`, `### Contents` use `### ` (H3) for the
        same reason as the Playbook hub — they are nested inside
        the `## Research hub` section, not section boundaries.

      Final emitted byte sequence (concatenation order):

      ```
      ---
      <frontmatter as today>
      ---

      ## Prose

      <existing prose body, unchanged>

      ## Playbook hub

      <playbook hub bytes from above, with nested `## ` → `### `>

      ## Research hub

      <research hub bytes from above, with nested `## ` → `### `>
      ```

      Each section's heading line is followed by a blank line and
      then the body bytes. The whole file ends with a trailing
      newline (matching the current template's end-of-file shape).

      Update the function's JSDoc to enumerate the three body
      sections and reference `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` as the
      format spec. Note explicitly that:
      - The Playbook hub bytes are the seed bytes the install
        service scaffolds as the playbook-hub artifact in the
        user's brain on cold install.
      - The Research hub bytes are the seed bytes the install
        service scaffolds as the research-hub artifact in the
        user's brain on cold install.
      - All body sections' bytes are user-editable post-scaffold;
        the template seeds them once, then the user owns them.
      - Nested headings inside any section MUST use `### ` or
        deeper (never `## `) so the parser's closed-vocabulary
        walker does not treat them as section boundaries.

### 2. Extend the test suite for the three-section body

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] Edit `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.test.ts`.
      Existing test groups are kept structurally; new test groups
      land for the new sections.

      - 2a. **Update the round-trip group ("round-trip parses
            cleanly under the new schema"):**
            - Existing assertion `expect(result.config.name).toBe(
              "obsidian")` stays.
            - Add: `expect(result.playbookHub.length).toBeGreaterThan(0)`
              and `expect(result.researchHub.length).toBeGreaterThan(0)`
              — both new sections must round-trip non-empty.

      - 2b. **Add a new test group: "three-section body shape".**
            - Assert `result.kind === "ok"` (proves the body grammar
              accepts the new template — guards against the parser
              regressing to single-section).
            - Assert each of `result.prose`, `result.playbookHub`,
              `result.researchHub` is a non-empty string.
            - Assert the three fields are byte-distinct from each
              other (no field accidentally aliased to another).

      - 2c. **Add a new test group: "Playbook hub section preserves
            sentinel structure".**
            - Assert `result.playbookHub` contains `# Coding Playbook`
              (the H1 title — proves the body bytes carried over
              intact from the legacy constant).
            - Assert `result.playbookHub` contains `### Task Routing`
              (proves nested headings were demoted to H3 per the
              closed-vocabulary rule).
            - Assert `result.playbookHub` does NOT contain
              `## Task Routing` (anchored to start-of-line via
              `/^## Task Routing$/m` — proves the demotion happened
              at the right level).
            - Assert `result.playbookHub` contains
              `[[your-conventions-note]]` and
              `[[your-folder-structure-note]]` (the seeded wikilinks).

      - 2d. **Add a new test group: "Research hub section ships
            structural seed".**
            - Assert `result.researchHub` contains `# Research`
              (the H1 title).
            - Assert `result.researchHub` contains `### Domains`
              (the canonical first sub-heading).
            - Assert `result.researchHub` contains `### Domain Hubs`.
            - Assert `result.researchHub` does NOT match
              `/^## Domains$/m`, `/^## Domain Hubs$/m`, or any other
              top-level `## ` heading (anchor with `^` and `$`
              against multi-line mode).

      - 2e. **Add a new test group: "no nested `## ` headings inside
            any body section".** This is the load-bearing
            closed-vocabulary regression test. Walk every body
            field (`prose`, `playbookHub`, `researchHub`) and
            assert it does NOT contain any `^## .+$` line — the
            ONLY top-level `## ` headings in the entire template
            output are the three section boundaries. If a future
            edit re-introduces a `## ` heading inside a section's
            body, this test fails and the parser would reject the
            template at runtime.

      - 2f. **Update the existing "Schema does not include
            intent-spec or deprecated fields" group** to confirm
            the frontmatter block extraction still works against
            the three-section template. The split heuristic
            (`content.split("\n---\n")[0]`) still isolates the
            frontmatter because the body sections do not contain
            `\n---\n` (the H3 horizontal rules use `---` on its
            own line surrounded by blank lines, but the heuristic
            anchors on `\n---\n` with no surrounding whitespace).
            Verify this assumption by adding an explicit
            assertion: `expect(content.split("\n---\n").length).
            toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(2)` — the split must produce at
            least frontmatter + body. If a future template change
            breaks this assumption, the assertion fires.

            BUT: the current Playbook hub bytes (and the new
            Research hub bytes) include `---` separators between
            sub-sections of the body. These will absolutely
            interfere with the naive split. Two options:
            - Option A: rewrite those sub-section separators in
              the bytes to use a different visual marker (e.g.
              omit them, or use blank lines only).
            - Option B: change the test's frontmatter-extraction
              heuristic to anchor on the FIRST `\n---\n` only:
              `content.indexOf("\n---\n")` → slice 0..that index.

            **Pick Option A.** The horizontal-rule separators in
            the legacy `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` are visual sugar,
            not load-bearing structure. Removing them keeps the
            template easier to edit and avoids forcing every test
            consumer to learn a non-obvious split heuristic. The
            implementor strips the `---` separator lines from
            both the Playbook hub bytes and the Research hub
            bytes when authoring step 1, leaving the H3 sub-
            headings to provide the visual structure on their
            own.

      - 2g. **Keep the platform-branching tests (Windows + POSIX)
            unchanged.** They assert on `command` and `args` —
            both are frontmatter, unaffected by the body
            widening.

      - 2h. **Keep the "interpolateArgs is a no-op on the default
            scaffold" tests unchanged.** Substitution surface is
            `mcpServerConfig.args` only; body sections are
            outside its scope.

      - 2i. **Keep the "prose body sentinel phrases preserved"
            tests unchanged.** They assert on `result.prose`,
            which is structurally isolated from the new sections.

### 3. Verify the template in isolation

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] 3a. Run `npm test -- obsidianBrainAideTemplate`. Every test
      in the file must pass. If any fails:
      - A round-trip failure (`result.kind === "malformed-body"`)
        indicates either (a) the parser's three-section grammar
        has not yet landed (`parseBrainAide`'s plan is the
        prerequisite — escalate) OR (b) the template body
        contains an undeclared top-level `## ` heading the
        parser rejects (find the offending line via the
        `unknown heading: <X>` reason and demote it to `### `).
      - A sentinel-content failure (`result.playbookHub` missing
        `[[your-conventions-note]]` etc.) indicates the byte
        copy from the legacy constant lost characters. Re-copy
        from `../index.ts`'s `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE`.

- [x] 3b. Run `npx tsc --noEmit`. The template helper has no
      type changes — typecheck is a sanity check that the
      JSDoc edits didn't break the imports. Downstream
      consumer (`provisionBrain`) will surface separate type
      errors because `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` and the CLAUDE.md
      template constants will be deleted in its plan; record
      those errors but do not fix them here.

## Decisions

- **Helper folder name stays `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/`.** The
  spec's strategy paragraph "The bundled-default-brain.aide
  template helper keeps its current name and folder" calls this
  out explicitly: the name describes the CONTENT (Obsidian-
  flavored prose, Obsidian-flavored hub bytes), not a code-level
  branch. The package never branches on Obsidian; the template
  ships Obsidian-flavored content because Obsidian is the most
  common storage choice. Renaming to `defaultBrainAideTemplate/`
  would obscure that the bytes are Obsidian-shaped, and would
  imply (incorrectly) that the package has a generic-default
  vs. backend-specific abstraction. There is none: this is the
  one default, and it is Obsidian-flavored.

- **Playbook hub bytes are CARRIED FORWARD from the legacy
  constant, not re-authored.** The current
  `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` constant in
  `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts` is the canonical
  source of those bytes — it has already been reviewed and
  accepted by the user as the seed shape. Re-authoring it in
  this plan would invite drift between "what the legacy install
  scaffolded" and "what the new install scaffolds." The
  implementor copies the constant's body verbatim into the
  template's `## Playbook hub` section, with one mechanical
  transformation: every nested `## ` heading becomes `### ` so
  the closed-vocabulary parser does not mistake them for
  section boundaries.

- **Research hub bytes are brand-new content, modeled on the
  Playbook hub's shape.** The user has not previously shipped
  a research hub seed; this plan is the first time that bytes
  land. The architectural decision is to mirror the Playbook
  hub's structural shape (H1 title, "How to Use This Index"
  guidance, table skeleton, empty placeholder list) so a user
  reading both seeds gets the same navigational pattern in two
  parallel surfaces. The Research hub specifically:
  - Uses `### Domains` as the entry table (mirroring `### Task
    Routing` on the Playbook hub).
  - Uses `### Domain Hubs` as the placeholder section users
    fill in with per-domain hub references (mirroring
    `### Sections` on the Playbook hub).
  - Includes a "How to Use This Index" guidance block that
    states the research-specific navigation rule: "drill into
    the domain that matches your task; do not flat-search
    across every domain."
  - Stays content-skeleton: zero domain-specific content, zero
    seeded entries. The user populates it with their own
    domains. The package never invents research content.

- **Nested `## ` headings inside body sections become `### `.**
  The parser's closed-vocabulary walker scans for `^## .+$` and
  treats every match as a section-boundary candidate. To make
  the template parse cleanly, every `## ` heading inside any
  section body must demote to `### ` (or deeper). This is a
  byte-level constraint imposed by the parser's body grammar;
  the template author and the parser author agree that
  top-level `## ` headings are reserved for section boundaries.
  The legacy `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` used `## ` for sub-section
  headings (a holdover from when the constant lived outside any
  enclosing section), so the demotion is mechanical: `## ` →
  `### ` everywhere inside both new sections. This is the
  load-bearing rule for any future addition to either body —
  documented in the test (step 2e) so a regression fires on
  reintroduction.

- **Visual `---` horizontal-rule separators are removed from
  the body bytes.** The legacy `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` used
  `\n---\n` between sub-sections as visual sugar. Carrying
  those separators forward would interfere with the
  frontmatter-extraction heuristic
  (`content.split("\n---\n")[0]`) the existing test suite uses
  to isolate the YAML block, and would also force every test
  consumer to learn a non-obvious split rule. The H3 sub-
  headings provide enough structure on their own; the
  separators are visual sugar that the user can re-add by hand
  if they want. Removing them at the seed level keeps the
  template simpler and the test suite simpler.

- **Frontmatter and prose body are unchanged.** The schema
  collapse already settled the frontmatter shape (`name` +
  `mcpServerConfig`); the prose body's sentinel phrases
  (`mcp__brain__read_note`, `mcp__brain__search_notes`,
  `CLAUDE.md`, `wikilink`) are still correct under the
  storage-agnostic identifier rule because they are CONTENT
  (Obsidian-flavored prose), not code identifiers. The
  identifier-naming rule applies to TypeScript names and
  inline string constants in code, not to template body
  content the user is supposed to read and edit. So the prose
  body carries forward verbatim.

- **The `## Prose` body keeps the wikilink-protocol guidance
  it currently has.** A separate consideration could be made
  about moving wikilink-crawling protocols out of the prose
  and into pipeline prompts (per the spec's storage-agnostic
  rule about methodology guidance). But that prose IS user-
  editable — the user can rewrite it the moment they decide
  their brain is not Obsidian. The package's job here is to
  ship a working Obsidian default, and the wikilink protocol
  is a load-bearing piece of how an Obsidian agent navigates
  the seeded brain. The methodology-belongs-in-prompts rule
  applies to CODE in `provisionBrain/index.ts` (which the
  separate provisionBrain plan addresses), not to TEMPLATE
  CONTENT in this helper.

- **No new helper subfolder.** The template emits a single
  string from a single function with platform-branched
  frontmatter and three byte-block sections. The current file
  is ~50 lines; the rewrite grows it to ~150 (frontmatter
  unchanged + ~50 lines of new section bytes each for
  Playbook and Research). Still well under the
  progressive-disclosure threshold for splitting. If a future
  refactor introduces per-section composition logic (e.g.
  reading section bodies from sibling files), factor each
  responsibility into its own subfolder at that point.
